<DocsLayout title="Mocks — Dokkimi Docs" description="Intercept outbound requests to external APIs and return controlled responses.">
  <h1>Mocks</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Intercept outbound HTTP requests from your services and return controlled responses.</p>

  <p>When a service makes an outbound HTTP request, the interceptor sidecar checks it against all MOCK items. If a mock matches, the mock response is returned. If no mock matches, the request passes through to its real destination. Mocks apply to every request: from services, workers, test steps and the UI test browser, to external hosts and to your own services.</p>

  <h2>Required fields</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>type</code></td><td><code>"MOCK"</code></td><td>Item type</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>string</td><td>Unique name (1-63 chars)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mockTarget</code></td><td>string</td><td>Target host, in any case (e.g., <code>api.stripe.com</code>), or the name of one of your services, or <code>*</code> for any. Matches any port unless it includes one; <code>:443</code> and <code>:80</code> match calls that leave it out. Subdomain wildcards are not supported.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mockPath</code></td><td>string</td><td>URL path to match (e.g., <code>/v1/charges</code>, <code>/api/*</code>). Characters can be written encoded or not (<code>/a%20b</code> or <code>/a b</code>), but <code>%2F</code> is not a <code>/</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Optional fields</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>description</code></td><td>string</td><td>—</td><td>Human-readable description (max 500 chars)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mockMethod</code></td><td>enum</td><td><code>*</code></td><td>HTTP method: <code>GET</code>, <code>POST</code>, <code>PUT</code>, <code>DELETE</code>, <code>PATCH</code>, <code>HEAD</code>, <code>OPTIONS</code>, or <code>*</code> (any)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mockOrigin</code></td><td>string</td><td>any</td><td>Name of the SERVICE or WORKER that makes the request. Omit it, or use <code>*</code>, for any caller, including test steps. Use <code>chromium</code> for the UI test browser.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mockDelayMs</code></td><td>integer</td><td>—</td><td>Artificial response delay in milliseconds. For a delay over 30 seconds, give a test step that waits for it a longer <code>timeout</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mockResponseStatus</code></td><td>integer</td><td>—</td><td>HTTP response status code (100-599)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mockResponseHeaders</code></td><td>object</td><td>—</td><td>Response headers. A mock with a body has <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> unless set here.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mockResponseBody</code></td><td>any JSON</td><td>—</td><td>Response body</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mockRequestBodyContains</code></td><td>string</td><td>—</td><td>Match only requests whose body contains this text (case-insensitive). Compressed bodies are decompressed first. Cannot combine with <code>mockRequestBodyMatches</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mockRequestBodyMatches</code></td><td>string</td><td>—</td><td>Match only requests whose body matches this Go regex. Case-sensitive; start with <code>(?i)</code> for case-insensitive. No lookaround or backreferences.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Example — mocking Stripe</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={`{
  "type": "MOCK",
  "name": "mock-stripe-charges",
  "mockMethod": "POST",
  "mockOrigin": "payment-service",
  "mockTarget": "api.stripe.com",
  "mockPath": "/v1/charges",
  "mockDelayMs": 100,
  "mockResponseStatus": 200,
  "mockResponseHeaders": { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  "mockResponseBody": {
    "id": "ch_test_123",
    "object": "charge",
    "amount": 2000,
    "currency": "usd",
    "status": "succeeded"
  }
}`} lang="json" />

  <h2>Example — mocking a failure</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={`{
  "type": "MOCK",
  "name": "mock-stripe-failure",
  "mockMethod": "POST",
  "mockTarget": "api.stripe.com",
  "mockPath": "/v1/charges",
  "mockResponseStatus": 402,
  "mockResponseBody": {
    "error": { "type": "card_error", "message": "Your card was declined." }
  }
}`} lang="json" />

  <h2>Path matching</h2>
  <p>The <code>mockPath</code> field supports several matching patterns:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Example</th><th>Matches</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Exact</td><td><code>/v1/charges</code></td><td>Only <code>/v1/charges</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Trailing wildcard</td><td><code>/api/*</code></td><td>Any path starting with <code>/api/</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Path variable (braces)</td><td><code>/api/users/&#123;userId&#125;</code></td><td><code>/api/users/123</code>, <code>/api/users/abc</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Path variable (colon)</td><td><code>/api/users/:id</code></td><td>Same as above — colon syntax is converted to braces internally</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Full wildcard</td><td><code>*</code></td><td>Any path</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Query strings are ignored during path matching. Wildcards in the middle of a path (e.g., <code>/api/*/posts</code>) are not supported — only trailing wildcards work — and neither are variables that are only part of a segment or combined with a wildcard. Those are validation errors, and so is a <code>=</code> in a variable (<code>&#123;name=**&#125;</code>).</p>
  <p>A trailing slash matters: <code>/api/users</code> doesn't match <code>/api/users/</code>, <code>/api/users/:id</code> doesn't match <code>/api/users/1/</code>, and <code>/api/*</code> doesn't match <code>/api</code>.</p>

  <h2 id="precedence">Mock precedence</h2>
  <p>A mock matches a request only if its target, origin, path, method, and body conditions all match. When several mocks match, they are compared field by field, in this order. A field only matters if the mocks are equal in those before it:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Target</strong> &mdash; host and port, then host, then <code>*</code></li>
    <li><strong>Origin</strong> &mdash; named, then omitted or <code>*</code></li>
    <li><strong>Path</strong> &mdash; exact, then with variables (fewer first), then the longer prefix before <code>*</code>, then <code>*</code></li>
    <li><strong>Method</strong> &mdash; named, then omitted or <code>*</code></li>
    <li><strong>Body</strong> &mdash; a body match, then none</li>
  </ol>
  <p>If they are still equal, the mock defined first wins. To return different responses for different bodies, give the mocks the same target, path, and method, and add one without a body match as the fallback.</p>

